<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Tortosa</h1><p class="st-meta">1009 CE – 1060 CE (approx.) · capital Tortosa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A taifa at the mouth of the Ebro ruled by former Slavic palace officials, prized for its port and shipyards before Zaragoza swallowed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba</a> collapsed in civil war after 1009, the saqaliba (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> of European origin) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amirids">Amirid</a> household seized several eastern towns. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> the freedman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labib-al-amiri">Labib al-Amiri</a> took power, reportedly for a time also sharing in the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> with Mubarak and Muzaffar.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> lived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> trade, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> from its hinterland and the shipyards that had built caliphal fleets. Its rulers, all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a>, lacked dynastic legitimacy and leaned on stronger neighbours such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, and on payments to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-barcelona">Counts of Barcelona</a>. Successive governors included Muqatil, Yali and Nabil.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1060 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hud">Hudid</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-muqtadir">al-Muqtadir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a>, ending its independence. The city stayed Muslim until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona</a> took it in 1148, after which it became a key port of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Catalonia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-tortosa.jpg" alt="Taifa of Tortosa" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: Tyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_Taifa_of_Tortosa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1009 CE – 1060 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tortosa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Labib al-Amiri, Muqatil, Nabil</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic, Mozarabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Tortosa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1034&amp;focus=taifa-of-tortosa" aria-label="Taifa of Tortosa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4693 720 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Tortosa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5013 976L4881 1071M5013 976L4979 949"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4881 1071h0M4979 949h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5013 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5013 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tortosa. <a href="/atlas?year=1034&amp;focus=taifa-of-tortosa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
